"json usage" is a notebook page
	<header level="1">
		JSON data
		<header>JSON data holds data in a page, as its text or from a file, and shows it as an outline that opens and closes.</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (attributes in practice, events, framework snippets), keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-json>` holds JSON data in a page, and shows it as an outline.

			- Its text is the data, or `source` loads it from a file on the same site.
			- `load` says when:  `eager` (at once), `visible` or `idle`.
			- `depth` says how many levels start open, `quotes="no"` drops the quotes round keys,
			  and `no-header` hides the Copy and quotes buttons.
			- `hidden` holds the data without showing it.
			- An element that takes JSON reads a `<ui-json>` child by itself, and keeps it out of sight.
			  `<ui-tree-diagram>` does, and follows it as it changes.

			A `<script type="application/json">` is the older way of holding data in a page.
			It works where it did, and inside a `<ui-json>` too.
			Use one there when the JSON holds a `<`, which a page would read as a tag.

			JSON as coloured text, with no folding, is `<ui-code language="json">`.

			In Spell it's `<json>`, and its text is kept as written:
			its `{` and `[` are JSON's, not Spell's.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Editing">
		<markdown>
			With `editable`, a double-click on a key or a value edits it, in a control that fits the value:

			- a string:  a text field, and it stays a string
			- a number:  a number field
			- a date (`2026-11-18`) or a date and time (`2026-11-18T10:00:00Z`):  a date picker,
			  and the new date is written the same way
			- `true` or `false`:  a checkbox, kept as soon as it's clicked
			- a key, or `null`:  a text field, read as JSON if it is, else as a string

			Enter, or leaving the field, keeps the edit;  Escape drops it.
			`value` changes, `ui-changed` says so, and the element is `dirty` until saved.

			JSON can't say a string comes from a list.
			`editors` can, by key, as a property:  a list makes a pick list, and a word names the control.

			```js
			const data = document.querySelector("ui-json")
			data.editors = { direction: ["in", "out", "inout"], made: "date" }
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="From script">
		<markdown>
			`value` is the data, parsed.
			It's `undefined` while `source` loads, and when the text is empty or isn't JSON.
			Set it to hold other data.  `ui-changed` says when it changed.

			```js
			const data = document.querySelector("ui-json")
			await data.loaded
			console.log(data.value)

			data.addEventListener("ui-changed", (event) => console.log(event.detail.value))
			data.value = { label: "New" }
			```

			Set `item` to show a thing:  its `data`, else the thing itself, becomes `value`.
			So slotted as a tree's `detail`, it shows each pick's data with no code:  the tree sets its `item`.

			A click on a key or a value sends `ui-navigating`, with where it is:
			`path`, the keys from the top down, then `key`, `value` and `part` (`"key"` or `"value"`).
			The page decides what it means, such as showing what a value names.

			```js
			data.addEventListener("ui-navigating", (event) => {
			  const { path, value } = event.detail
			  if (path.at(-1) === "id") showThing(value)
			})
			```

			`copy()` puts the data on the clipboard as JSON, as the Copy button does, and sends `ui-copied`.

			Text that isn't JSON shows why, logs one console warning, sends `ui-error` and sets `:state(error)`.
			So does a file that won't load.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			Each object's and array's toggle is a real button:  it says what it opens, and whether it's open.
			Each one is a Tab stop;  the arrow keys don't move between lines yet.
			A hidden `<ui-json>`, or one a diagram reads, shows nothing, and screen readers pass over it.
		</>
	</>
